I think you've got a basic confusion going on here
between what the PC remembers and what the player
remembers. NetHack is only interested in messing up
what the PC remembers.

I fail to understand how "keeping good notes" of
_any_ information the game makes readily available
to you can constitute "cheating".

Taking good notes just sounds like "due diligence"
to me, the difference in caring about
winning/surviving between searching obsessively and
thus never falling into a trap, versus blithely
walking into every trap in your path depending on
luck and hit points to keep you from expiring.

Those are "game play style" differences, not cheats.

Just because NetHack is designed to litter levels
with traps _doesn't_ mean that you the player are
_required_ to guide "you" the PC into stepping on
each of them in turn.

Just because NetHack is designed to make "you" the
PC forget stuff doesn't require that _you the
player_ be similarly forbidden to remember that
same stuff, given that you as a player have had the
fair "in-band" opportunity to learn that "stuff"
once.

IMO

xanthian.

YANI: combine the "object amnesia" effect with a
"randomize B/U/C statue" effect covering the same
objects, perhaps exempting objects currently worn.
Even with just a small chance (10%, say) of changing
the B/U/C status of any particular object, and even
with the original random object distribution of
B/U/C status as a result, this would make amnesia
attacks or scrolls massively more evil.


